/* CSO Cyber Range — Course content: BC-108 Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI) Fundamentals */
window.COURSE_DATA = window.COURSE_DATA || {};
window.COURSE_DATA["BC-108"] = {
ov:"Threat intelligence turns raw data about adversaries into decisions that improve defense. This course builds the CTI discipline — the intelligence lifecycle, the frameworks that model adversary behavior (Kill Chain, ATT&CK, Diamond), indicators and attribution, and how to operationalize intelligence so it actually changes what defenders do. Each module ends in a required knowledge check; the 40-item capstone draws two items per module plus applied scenarios.",
mods:[
{t:"The Intelligence Lifecycle",d:"From requirements to dissemination and back.",
obj:["Name the phases of the intelligence lifecycle","Explain how requirements drive the whole cycle","Explain why intelligence is a cycle, not a one-way pipeline"],
read:["Cyber threat intelligence follows a lifecycle: requirements (what questions must be answered), collection (gathering relevant data), processing (turning raw data into usable form), analysis (deriving meaning and assessments), production (packaging for the audience), dissemination (delivering to those who act), and feedback (which refines the next requirements). Each phase feeds the next, and the cycle is driven from the front by requirements — intelligence exists to answer specific questions that matter to defenders and decision-makers, not to collect data for its own sake.",
"The lifecycle framing matters because it disciplines the work. Without clear requirements, collection becomes hoarding and analysis becomes aimless; with them, every phase is purposeful. And because it is a cycle, not a pipeline, feedback closes the loop: consumers tell producers what was useful and what new questions arose, refining future requirements. A CTI program that skips requirements and feedback drowns in feeds and produces reports no one uses — the common failure this discipline exists to prevent."],
ex:"A SOC lead sets a requirement: which ransomware groups target our sector and how do they gain initial access? This drives collection (sector threat reports, ISAC feeds), analysis (mapping the groups' techniques), production (a briefing), and dissemination (to the defense teams), then feedback (the teams ask a sharper follow-up). The requirement kept the effort focused on a question that changed defensive priorities — the lifecycle working as designed.",
refs:[["Recorded Future / Mandiant, intelligence lifecycle overviews","The CTI lifecycle phases and their purpose."],["NIST SP 800-150","Guide to cyber threat information sharing."]],
kc:[
{q:"The intelligence lifecycle is driven from the front by:",o:["Collection volume","Requirements — the specific questions intelligence must answer","Dissemination","The number of feeds"],a:1,x:"Requirements give the cycle purpose; without them collection becomes hoarding and analysis becomes aimless."},
{q:"Intelligence is described as a cycle rather than a pipeline because:",o:["It never ends by accident","Feedback from consumers refines the next round of requirements, closing the loop","It is circular in shape","Collection repeats randomly"],a:1,x:"Feedback tells producers what was useful and what new questions arose, continuously sharpening future requirements."},
{q:"A CTI program that skips requirements and feedback tends to:",o:["Produce highly targeted intelligence","Drown in feeds and produce reports no one uses","Save money","Improve attribution"],a:1,x:"Without requirements to focus it and feedback to refine it, the program collects indiscriminately and delivers little decision value."}]},
{t:"Strategic, Operational, and Tactical Intelligence",d:"Matching intelligence to its audience and time horizon.",
obj:["Distinguish strategic, operational, and tactical intelligence","Match each level to its audience and use","Explain why level confusion produces useless products"],
read:["Threat intelligence operates at three levels distinguished by audience and time horizon. Strategic intelligence is high-level and long-term, for executives and decision-makers — threat landscape trends, adversary motivations, geopolitical risk, and their business implications, informing investment and policy. Operational intelligence concerns specific campaigns and adversary behavior — who is targeting your sector, what their tactics are — informing defensive priorities and hunting. Tactical intelligence is immediate and technical — indicators, signatures, specific techniques — feeding detection tools and responders directly.",
"Matching the level to the audience is essential, because the same finding must be expressed differently for different consumers. An executive needs the strategic implication (a nation-state actor is increasingly targeting our industry, warranting investment in detection); a SOC analyst needs the tactical detail (block these hashes, hunt for this technique). Delivering raw indicators to an executive or a geopolitical essay to a SOC analyst wastes both. The level confusion — producing tactical detail for strategic audiences or vice versa — is a common failure that makes otherwise-good intelligence useless to its recipient."],
ex:"Analysis of an APT campaign yields output at all three levels: strategic (a briefing to leadership on the actor's sustained interest in the sector, supporting a detection-investment decision), operational (a profile of the campaign's tactics for the hunt team), and tactical (IOCs pushed to the SIEM). Each audience receives the level they can act on — the same intelligence, correctly pitched, drives decisions at every tier.",
refs:[["Mandiant / SANS CTI, levels of intelligence","Strategic, operational, and tactical intelligence and their audiences."],["NIST SP 800-150","Sharing intelligence across organizational levels."]],
kc:[
{q:"Strategic threat intelligence is aimed primarily at:",o:["SOC analysts, for immediate blocking","Executives and decision-makers, informing long-term investment and policy","Firewalls","Nobody"],a:1,x:"Strategic intelligence covers trends, motivations, and business implications for leadership decisions — not immediate technical action."},
{q:"Tactical intelligence is characterized by:",o:["Geopolitical analysis","Immediate, technical detail — indicators and signatures — feeding detection and responders","Multi-year forecasts","Budget recommendations"],a:1,x:"Tactical intelligence is the technical, actionable layer (IOCs, techniques) that tools and responders use directly."},
{q:"Delivering raw IOCs to an executive is a failure of:",o:["Collection","Matching the intelligence level to the audience — the executive needs the strategic implication","Attribution","Encryption"],a:1,x:"Level confusion wastes good intelligence; each audience must receive the level (strategic/operational/tactical) they can act on."}]},
{t:"Data, Information, and Intelligence",d:"The processing that adds value.",
obj:["Distinguish data, information, and intelligence","Explain the analytic value added at each step","Explain why raw feeds are not intelligence"],
read:["A core distinction underlies the whole discipline: data is raw observations (an IP address, a hash, a log line); information is data given context (this IP contacted our server 400 times last night); intelligence is analyzed information that answers a question and supports a decision (this IP is C2 infrastructure for a group targeting our sector; block it and hunt for related activity). Each step adds analytic value — context, correlation, assessment — transforming inert observations into something a defender can act on with confidence.",
"This distinction explains why a raw threat feed is not intelligence. A list of a million IP addresses with no context, confidence, or relevance to your environment is data; dumping it into a blocklist produces false positives and no understanding. Intelligence requires the analytic work of processing and analysis — enriching, correlating, assessing confidence, and tying findings to your specific requirements. The discipline's value is precisely this transformation; organizations that equate buying feeds with having intelligence have skipped the step that creates the value."],
ex:"A feed delivers 50,000 IPs (data). Enriched with context — which are currently active C2, which relate to actors targeting the sector, which have ever appeared in the organization's own logs (information) — and analyzed for relevance and confidence, a small, high-value subset emerges as actionable intelligence. The raw feed alone would have generated noise; the analytic transformation produced decisions.",
refs:[["SANS CTI, data-information-intelligence","The transformation that defines the discipline."],["Recorded Future, intelligence fundamentals","Adding context and analysis to raw data."]],
kc:[
{q:"The difference between data and intelligence is that intelligence is:",o:["Larger in volume","Analyzed information that answers a question and supports a decision","Always an IP address","Unprocessed"],a:1,x:"Data is raw observation; intelligence adds context, correlation, and assessment to enable confident action."},
{q:"A raw threat feed of a million IPs with no context is:",o:["Finished intelligence","Data — it requires processing and analysis to become intelligence","Always actionable","A strategic product"],a:1,x:"Without context, relevance, and confidence, a feed is inert data; equating feeds with intelligence skips the value-adding step."},
{q:"The analytic value added in turning data into intelligence includes:",o:["Only deleting duplicates","Context, correlation, confidence assessment, and relevance to specific requirements","Encrypting the data","Increasing the volume"],a:1,x:"Processing and analysis enrich, correlate, and assess data against requirements — the transformation that creates decision value."}]},
{t:"The Cyber Kill Chain",d:"Modeling an intrusion as sequential stages.",
obj:["Describe the phases of the Lockheed Martin Cyber Kill Chain","Explain how the model informs defensive intervention","Discuss the model's strengths and limitations"],
read:["The Lockheed Martin Cyber Kill Chain models an intrusion as seven sequential stages: reconnaissance, weaponization, delivery, exploitation, installation, command and control, and actions on objectives. Its defensive value is the insight that an attacker must complete every stage to succeed, so defenders can break the chain at any stage — detecting and disrupting delivery, blocking C2, or catching lateral movement each defeats the intrusion. This maps defensive controls to attack stages and encourages depth: multiple chances to break the chain (the defense-in-depth logic of BC-101).",
"The model's strength is its clarity and the intervention mindset it instills; its limitations are that it is linear and perimeter-focused, fitting classic malware intrusions better than insider threats, supply-chain compromises, or attacks that skip stages. Modern practice often pairs the Kill Chain with ATT&CK (next module), using the Kill Chain for the high-level narrative and ATT&CK for the detailed technique mapping. Understanding the Kill Chain gives you the vocabulary of intrusion stages that the rest of the discipline builds on."],
ex:"Analyzing a phishing intrusion via the Kill Chain: reconnaissance (LinkedIn targeting), weaponization (malicious document), delivery (email), exploitation (macro execution), installation (backdoor), C2 (beaconing), actions on objectives (data theft). The team maps a defensive control to each stage and realizes their C2-blocking egress filter is the strongest link — the model showed exactly where their existing defenses break the chain and where the gaps are.",
refs:[["Lockheed Martin, Cyber Kill Chain","The seven-stage intrusion model and its defensive application."],["MITRE ATT&CK","The complementary technique-level framework."]],
kc:[
{q:"The central defensive insight of the Cyber Kill Chain is that:",o:["Attackers always succeed","An attacker must complete every stage, so breaking the chain at any stage defeats the intrusion","Only the last stage matters","Perimeter defense is sufficient"],a:1,x:"Because the stages are sequential and all required, defenders get multiple chances to disrupt — the depth logic of BC-101."},
{q:"A limitation of the Cyber Kill Chain is that it:",o:["Has too many stages","Is linear and perimeter-focused, fitting classic malware better than insider or supply-chain attacks","Cannot map controls","Is only strategic"],a:1,x:"Its linear, perimeter model fits traditional intrusions; modern attacks that skip stages or come from inside fit it less well."},
{q:"Modern practice often pairs the Kill Chain with ATT&CK to:",o:["Replace both","Use the Kill Chain for high-level narrative and ATT&CK for detailed technique mapping","Avoid frameworks","Simplify to one stage"],a:1,x:"The Kill Chain gives the intrusion story; ATT&CK provides the granular technique detail — complementary lenses."}]},
{t:"The MITRE ATT&CK Framework",d:"A knowledge base of adversary tactics and techniques.",
obj:["Explain what ATT&CK is and how it is structured","Distinguish tactics, techniques, and procedures","Describe using ATT&CK for detection and gap analysis"],
read:["MITRE ATT&CK is a curated, empirically-derived knowledge base of adversary behavior, organized as tactics (the adversary's goals — initial access, persistence, lateral movement, exfiltration) and techniques (how they achieve each goal — spearphishing, valid accounts, pass-the-hash). It is the common language for describing what attackers actually do, grounded in observed real-world behavior rather than theory. Each technique includes detection guidance, mitigations, and the groups known to use it, making it directly useful to defenders.",
"ATT&CK's power for defenders is coverage mapping and gap analysis. By mapping their detections and controls to ATT&CK techniques, teams see which adversary behaviors they can detect and which they cannot — turning defense from a vague sense of coverage into a concrete map of strengths and blind spots. It also structures threat hunting (which techniques should we hunt for?) and drives detection engineering (build a detection for this uncovered technique). The tactics-techniques-procedures (TTP) framing connects to the Pyramid of Pain (module seven): TTPs are the hardest indicators for adversaries to change, so detections built on ATT&CK techniques are durable."],
ex:"A SOC maps its detection rules to the ATT&CK matrix and discovers strong coverage of initial-access and execution techniques but almost none for credential access and lateral movement — precisely the stages an APT would use post-compromise. The gap analysis redirects detection engineering to the blind spots, and a new detection for pass-the-hash (a credential-access technique) closes a critical gap the team did not know it had.",
refs:[["MITRE ATT&CK","The tactics, techniques, and knowledge base itself."],["MITRE, ATT&CK for defenders and DeTT&CT","Using ATT&CK for coverage mapping and detection engineering."]],
kc:[
{q:"In MITRE ATT&CK, tactics represent the adversary's ___ and techniques represent ___:",o:["Tools; budgets","Goals (e.g., persistence); how they achieve each goal (e.g., valid accounts)","Names; locations","Signatures; hashes"],a:1,x:"Tactics are the why (the objective) and techniques are the how — the structured vocabulary of adversary behavior."},
{q:"ATT&CK is especially valuable to defenders for:",o:["Blocking IP addresses","Coverage mapping and gap analysis — seeing which adversary behaviors they can and cannot detect","Encrypting data","Writing policy"],a:1,x:"Mapping detections to techniques turns vague coverage into a concrete map of detection strengths and blind spots."},
{q:"Detections built on ATT&CK techniques (TTPs) are durable because TTPs are:",o:["Easy for adversaries to change","The hardest indicators for adversaries to change, unlike hashes or IPs","Always encrypted","Irrelevant to attackers"],a:1,x:"Per the Pyramid of Pain, behavioral techniques are costly for adversaries to alter, so technique-based detections resist evasion."}]},
{t:"The Diamond Model",d:"Adversary, capability, infrastructure, victim.",
obj:["Describe the four core features of the Diamond Model","Explain how the model structures intrusion analysis","Explain pivoting across the diamond"],
read:["The Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis frames each intrusion event around four connected features: the adversary (who), their capability (the tools and techniques), the infrastructure (the systems they use — C2 servers, domains), and the victim (the target). The edges connect them — an adversary uses a capability over infrastructure against a victim — and analyzing these relationships structures how you reason about an intrusion, ensuring you consider all four dimensions rather than fixating on one.",
"The model's practical power is pivoting: knowing one feature lets you discover others. From a piece of malware (capability) you can pivot to the infrastructure it contacts; from that infrastructure you can pivot to other victims it has targeted or the adversary that controls it. This is how analysts expand from a single observation into a full picture of a campaign. The Diamond Model complements ATT&CK (which describes capability in detail) and the Kill Chain (which sequences events) — together they give analysts multiple structured lenses on the same intrusion."],
ex:"An analyst starts with a malicious domain (infrastructure) found in their logs. Pivoting, they find the malware that contacts it (capability), which ATT&CK maps to a known group (adversary), whose infrastructure has also targeted three peer organizations (other victims). One observed feature expanded, via the diamond's edges, into a campaign-level understanding that drove sector-wide warning.",
refs:[["Caltagirone, Pendergast & Betz, The Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis","The foundational paper defining the model and pivoting."],["SANS CTI, Diamond Model application","Using the model in practical intrusion analysis."]],
kc:[
{q:"The four core features of the Diamond Model are:",o:["People, process, technology, budget","Adversary, capability, infrastructure, victim","Confidentiality, integrity, availability, utility","Plan, do, check, act"],a:1,x:"The model links adversary, capability, infrastructure, and victim — the dimensions of every intrusion event."},
{q:"Pivoting in the Diamond Model means:",o:["Rotating the diagram","Using a known feature to discover connected features — e.g., from malware to its infrastructure to the adversary","Deleting a feature","Encrypting the analysis"],a:1,x:"The edges connect features, so one observation (e.g., a domain) leads to others, expanding a single event into a campaign picture."},
{q:"The Diamond Model complements ATT&CK and the Kill Chain by:",o:["Replacing them","Providing another structured lens — relationship and pivoting — alongside technique detail and event sequencing","Being the only valid model","Focusing only on victims"],a:1,x:"The three frameworks together give sequencing (Kill Chain), technique detail (ATT&CK), and relationship/pivoting (Diamond)."}]},
{t:"Indicators and the Pyramid of Pain",d:"Not all indicators are equal.",
obj:["Distinguish types of indicators of compromise","Explain the Pyramid of Pain and its ordering","Explain why behavioral indicators are more valuable"],
read:["Indicators of compromise (IOCs) are observable artifacts of an intrusion, and they vary enormously in value. The Pyramid of Pain ranks them by how much pain detecting them causes the adversary — meaning how hard they are for the attacker to change. At the bottom: hash values (trivial to change — one byte alters the hash), IP addresses, and domain names (easy to rotate). In the middle: network and host artifacts, tools. At the top: tactics, techniques, and procedures (TTPs) — the adversary's behavior, which is expensive and difficult to change because it reflects how they operate.",
"The strategic implication is to build detection on higher-pyramid indicators. Blocking a hash stops exactly one file; the adversary recompiles and defeats it in seconds. Detecting a TTP — the behavior of, say, credential dumping or a specific persistence method — catches the adversary regardless of which specific file or IP they use, because changing the behavior is costly. This is why ATT&CK-based, behavioral detection is durable while IOC blocklists are perishable. Both have their place — hashes and IPs are cheap, immediate blocks — but a mature program invests in the top of the pyramid where detection is hardest for the adversary to evade."],
ex:"A team blocks a malware hash and the IP its sample contacts. The adversary recompiles the malware (new hash) and moves to new infrastructure (new IP) within hours, defeating both blocks. But the team also built an ATT&CK-based detection for the credential-dumping technique the malware used — and that detection catches the adversary's next sample too, because they kept the same behavior. The high-pyramid detection endured where the low ones perished.",
refs:[["David Bianco, The Pyramid of Pain","The indicator hierarchy and its detection implications."],["MITRE ATT&CK","The TTP layer at the top of the pyramid."]],
kc:[
{q:"In the Pyramid of Pain, which indicator is hardest for an adversary to change?",o:["A file hash","An IP address","A domain name","Tactics, techniques, and procedures (TTPs)"],a:1,x:"TTPs reflect how the adversary operates; changing behavior is far costlier than recompiling a file or rotating an IP."},
{q:"Blocking a malware hash is low-value detection because:",o:["Hashes are secret","The adversary can recompile to change the hash in seconds, defeating the block","Hashes cannot be blocked","It causes false positives"],a:1,x:"A one-byte change alters the hash; hash blocks stop exactly one file and are trivially evaded."},
{q:"Behavioral (TTP-based) detection is more durable because:",o:["It is cheaper to build","Changing the underlying behavior is expensive for the adversary, so the detection survives their tool and infrastructure changes","It never false-positives","It requires no tuning"],a:1,x:"Detecting behavior catches the adversary regardless of the specific file or IP, since altering behavior is the costliest change."}]},
{t:"Threat Actor Attribution",d:"Who did it — and the confidence and caveats.",
obj:["Explain the basis and difficulty of attribution","Describe attribution confidence levels and their communication","Explain the operational value and risks of attribution"],
read:["Attribution assigns an intrusion to a responsible actor, drawing on TTPs, infrastructure overlaps, code similarities, timing, targeting patterns, and language or operational artifacts. It is genuinely difficult and uncertain: sophisticated actors use false flags, shared tools, and rented infrastructure to obscure or misdirect. Responsible attribution therefore comes with explicit confidence levels (low/moderate/high) and stated evidence, never as unqualified certainty. Overconfident attribution — naming an actor without caveats — is a common and damaging analytic failure.",
"Attribution has real operational value when done well: knowing which actor you face informs the adversary hypothesis (as in BC-101's retailer-versus-defense-contractor example), predicts likely next moves, and prioritizes defenses against that actor's known TTPs. But it also carries risk — misattribution can misdirect defense, cause diplomatic or business harm, and erode credibility. The professional stance is to attribute at the level the evidence supports, communicate confidence honestly, and remember that for most defensive decisions, understanding the behavior (TTPs) matters more than naming the actor. Attribution serves defense; it is not a trophy."],
ex:"An analyst assesses with moderate confidence that an intrusion aligns with a known group, citing TTP and infrastructure overlaps, while noting the caveat that another group uses similar tools. This calibrated statement lets defenders prioritize against the likely actor without overcommitting. An unqualified this was definitely Group X — later proven wrong — would have misdirected the response and damaged the team's credibility.",
refs:[["Mandiant / CrowdStrike attribution methodology","How TTPs, infrastructure, and code inform attribution and confidence."],["Rid & Buchanan, Attributing Cyber Attacks","The analytic and evidentiary basis of attribution."]],
kc:[
{q:"Responsible threat attribution is always communicated with:",o:["Absolute certainty","Explicit confidence levels and stated evidence, acknowledging uncertainty","No supporting evidence","The actor's home address"],a:1,x:"Sophisticated actors obscure and misdirect; honest attribution states confidence and evidence rather than unqualified certainty."},
{q:"For most defensive decisions, what matters more than naming the actor?",o:["The actor's nationality","Understanding the behavior (TTPs) so defenses can be prioritized against how they operate","The actor's logo","The exact hash"],a:1,x:"Defenders act on behavior; knowing the TTPs improves defense whether or not the actor is definitively named."},
{q:"Overconfident attribution is damaging because it can:",o:["Improve defense","Misdirect defense, cause diplomatic or business harm, and erode analytic credibility","Never be wrong","Speed up detection"],a:1,x:"Naming an actor beyond the evidence risks misdirected response and lost credibility when the attribution proves wrong."}]},
{t:"STIX, TAXII, and Intelligence Sharing",d:"Standardizing and exchanging threat intelligence.",
obj:["Explain what STIX and TAXII provide","Describe the value and models of intelligence sharing","Explain trust and handling considerations in sharing"],
read:["Threat intelligence is more valuable shared, and standards make sharing scalable. STIX (Structured Threat Information Expression) is a standardized language for representing threat intelligence — indicators, TTPs, actors, relationships — so that intelligence produced by one organization or tool can be consumed by another without manual translation. TAXII (Trusted Automated Exchange of Intelligence Information) is the transport protocol for exchanging STIX data. Together they let organizations, ISACs, and vendors share machine-readable intelligence automatically at scale.",
"Sharing multiplies defensive value: an indicator or TTP discovered by one organization warns many, and sector sharing communities (ISACs/ISAOs) turn individual observations into collective defense. But sharing requires trust and careful handling — the Traffic Light Protocol (TLP) marks how widely information may be shared (from TLP:RED, named recipients only, to TLP:CLEAR, public), and sensitive details (victim identity, sources) must be protected. Effective sharing balances openness (more shared intelligence helps everyone) against the risks of exposing victims, tipping off adversaries, or leaking sensitive methods — a governance discipline as much as a technical one."],
ex:"An organization detects a new phishing campaign and shares the indicators and TTPs as STIX via its sector ISAC's TAXII feed, marked TLP:AMBER (share within the community, not publicly). Member organizations automatically ingest the intelligence and block the campaign before it reaches them — one detection became collective defense, enabled by the standards and the trust-marked sharing.",
refs:[["OASIS STIX/TAXII specifications","The standards for representing and exchanging threat intelligence."],["FIRST Traffic Light Protocol (TLP)","Markings governing how shared intelligence may be redistributed."]],
kc:[
{q:"STIX and TAXII respectively provide:",o:["Encryption and firewalls","A standardized language for threat intelligence (STIX) and a protocol to exchange it (TAXII)","Two antivirus engines","Password policies"],a:1,x:"STIX structures the intelligence; TAXII transports it — together enabling automated, scalable machine-readable sharing."},
{q:"The Traffic Light Protocol (TLP) is used to:",o:["Rate malware severity","Mark how widely shared intelligence may be redistributed (e.g., TLP:RED to TLP:CLEAR)","Encrypt STIX data","Attribute actors"],a:1,x:"TLP governs redistribution, balancing the value of sharing against protecting sensitive details and victims."},
{q:"Intelligence sharing multiplies defensive value because:",o:["It reduces the amount of intelligence","One organization's discovery can warn and protect many others","It eliminates the need for analysis","It hides indicators"],a:1,x:"Sharing turns individual observations into collective defense, especially through sector communities (ISACs/ISAOs)."}]},
{t:"Open-Source Intelligence (OSINT)",d:"Gathering from public sources responsibly.",
obj:["Explain what OSINT is and its sources","Describe OSINT's value for defense and its use by adversaries","Explain legal and ethical boundaries of OSINT"],
read:["Open-Source Intelligence is intelligence derived from publicly available sources — threat reports, vendor blogs, security researchers, public malware repositories, code repositories, social media, DNS and certificate transparency logs, and paste sites. OSINT is a cornerstone of CTI because so much valuable information is public: newly-disclosed vulnerabilities, actor TTPs published by researchers, leaked credentials appearing in breach dumps, and infrastructure revealed in certificate logs. A skilled analyst extracts substantial intelligence without any privileged access.",
"OSINT is dual-use: the same techniques defenders use to research threats, adversaries use to research targets — enumerating an organization's employees for spearphishing, finding exposed services, and harvesting leaked credentials. Understanding OSINT thus serves both offense-awareness (knowing what an attacker can learn about you, which informs your attack surface reduction) and defense (researching adversaries). It also has boundaries: OSINT gathers what is public, but the ethical and legal line is real — accessing non-public data, even if poorly protected, or using pretexting to extract information, crosses from OSINT into intrusion or social engineering."],
ex:"An analyst uses certificate transparency logs and public DNS data to map a threat actor's infrastructure, and a breach-dump search to find which of the organization's credentials have leaked — all from public sources. The same OSINT lens reveals what an attacker could learn about the organization (exposed subdomains, employee emails), driving both threat research and attack-surface reduction.",
refs:[["SANS / Bellingcat OSINT methodology","Techniques and sources for open-source intelligence."],["OSINT Framework","A catalog of open-source collection sources and tools."]],
kc:[
{q:"OSINT is intelligence derived from:",o:["Classified sources only","Publicly available sources — reports, repositories, DNS/certificate logs, social media, breach dumps","Internal logs only","Paid feeds exclusively"],a:1,x:"OSINT extracts value from public information; much valuable intelligence requires no privileged access."},
{q:"OSINT is dual-use because:",o:["It is always illegal","The same public-research techniques defenders use, adversaries use to research targets","It only helps attackers","It requires special hardware"],a:1,x:"Attackers OSINT their targets (employees, exposed services, leaked creds); understanding this informs your own attack-surface reduction."},
{q:"The ethical and legal boundary of OSINT is crossed when an analyst:",o:["Reads a public blog","Accesses non-public data or uses pretexting to extract information","Searches certificate transparency logs","Reads a threat report"],a:1,x:"OSINT gathers public information; accessing protected data or deceiving people to extract it becomes intrusion or social engineering."}]},
{t:"Analytic Techniques and Cognitive Bias",d:"Rigorous analysis and avoiding predictable errors.",
obj:["Describe structured analytic techniques","Identify cognitive biases that distort analysis","Explain how confidence and assumptions should be expressed"],
read:["Analysis is where data becomes intelligence, and doing it rigorously means guarding against the predictable ways human judgment errs. Structured analytic techniques — such as the Analysis of Competing Hypotheses, where an analyst explicitly lists alternative explanations and weighs evidence against each rather than fixating on a first impression — impose discipline that counters bias. Explicitly stating assumptions and confidence levels, and identifying what evidence would change the assessment, keeps analysis honest and reviewable.",
"The biases to guard against are well-catalogued. Confirmation bias leads analysts to favor evidence supporting their initial theory and dismiss contradicting evidence. Anchoring fixes judgment on the first information seen. Mirror-imaging assumes the adversary thinks as the analyst would. Availability bias overweights recent or memorable events. These are not signs of poor analysts but universal human tendencies, which is exactly why structured techniques and peer review exist — to catch the errors that individual intuition reliably makes. An assessment that states its confidence, assumptions, and alternative hypotheses is far more trustworthy than a confident narrative that hides them."],
ex:"An analyst initially believes an intrusion is a known group (anchoring on the first indicator). Applying Analysis of Competing Hypotheses, they list alternatives and find the evidence actually fits a second group better once a false-flag artifact is discounted. The structured technique overrode the anchoring bias that a confident first-impression narrative would have cemented — producing a more accurate, caveated assessment.",
refs:[["Richards Heuer, Psychology of Intelligence Analysis","Cognitive biases and structured analytic techniques."],["CIA, A Tradecraft Primer: Structured Analytic Techniques","Practical techniques including Analysis of Competing Hypotheses."]],
kc:[
{q:"Confirmation bias in analysis leads an analyst to:",o:["Weigh all evidence equally","Favor evidence supporting their initial theory and dismiss contradicting evidence","Always change their mind","Ignore the first indicator"],a:1,x:"Confirmation bias distorts toward the initial hypothesis; structured techniques counter it by forcing consideration of alternatives."},
{q:"Analysis of Competing Hypotheses improves rigor by:",o:["Picking the first explanation","Explicitly listing alternative explanations and weighing evidence against each","Ignoring evidence","Increasing confidence automatically"],a:1,x:"By evaluating multiple hypotheses against the evidence, it counters anchoring and confirmation bias for a more defensible assessment."},
{q:"A trustworthy analytic assessment explicitly states:",o:["Only the conclusion","Its confidence level, assumptions, and what evidence would change it","The analyst's name only","Nothing about uncertainty"],a:1,x:"Stating confidence, assumptions, and disconfirming evidence makes analysis honest, reviewable, and resistant to hidden bias."}]},
{t:"Operationalizing CTI for Defense",d:"Turning intelligence into detections and decisions.",
obj:["Explain how CTI feeds detection, hunting, and response","Describe intelligence-driven defense","Explain measuring CTI value by defensive impact"],
read:["Intelligence has value only when it changes what defenders do. Operationalizing CTI means feeding it into concrete defensive functions: tactical indicators and TTPs become detection rules and hunt hypotheses (BC-102's baselining and the hunting of IC-205); operational intelligence prioritizes which threats to defend against and which techniques to detect first; strategic intelligence guides investment. Intelligence-driven defense uses knowledge of the adversary to focus finite defensive resources where they matter most — hunting for the techniques your actual threats use, not every conceivable one.",
"The measure of a CTI program is defensive impact, not report volume. A program that produces beautiful reports no one acts on has failed; one whose intelligence results in new detections that fire on real threats, hunts that find intrusions, and investment decisions that close real gaps has succeeded. This connects to the metrics discipline from BC-101 — measure CTI by outcomes (detections created, threats caught, decisions informed), not by activity (feeds ingested, reports written). The whole lifecycle, from requirements through dissemination, exists to produce this operational impact, and feedback closes the loop by revealing which intelligence actually changed defense."],
ex:"A CTI team's analysis of a ransomware group targeting the sector produces three ATT&CK-mapped detections (deployed to the SIEM), two hunt hypotheses (one of which finds an unrelated dormant intrusion), and a briefing that wins funding for MFA rollout. The program's value is measured by these outcomes — a caught intrusion and a closed gap — not by the report's length. Intelligence changed defense, which is the entire point.",
refs:[["MITRE ATT&CK and intelligence-driven defense","Mapping intelligence to detections and hunts."],["SANS, intelligence-driven incident response","Operationalizing CTI across defensive functions."]],
kc:[
{q:"Operationalizing CTI means:",o:["Producing more reports","Feeding intelligence into detections, hunts, prioritization, and investment — changing what defenders do","Collecting more feeds","Attributing every attack"],a:1,x:"Intelligence has value only when it alters defensive action; operationalizing turns it into detections, hunts, and decisions."},
{q:"Intelligence-driven defense focuses finite resources by:",o:["Defending against every conceivable technique equally","Hunting for and detecting the techniques your actual threats use","Ignoring the adversary","Buying more tools"],a:1,x:"Knowledge of the real adversary concentrates defensive effort where it matters, rather than spreading it thin over all possibilities."},
{q:"A CTI program should be measured by:",o:["The number of feeds ingested and reports written","Defensive impact — detections created, threats caught, decisions informed","Report length","Attribution certainty"],a:1,x:"Outcomes, not activity, measure CTI value; beautiful reports no one acts on are a failure regardless of volume."}]},
{t:"Collection Management and Requirements",d:"Turning questions into a collection plan.",
obj:["Explain how requirements decompose into collection needs","Describe a collection management framework","Explain prioritizing and de-conflicting collection"],
read:["Between a requirement and useful analysis sits collection management: the deliberate planning of what to collect, from where, to answer the question. A broad requirement (understand ransomware risk to our sector) decomposes into specific collection needs (which groups are active, their initial-access techniques, their infrastructure patterns), each mapped to sources that can satisfy it (ISAC reporting, vendor intelligence, internal telemetry, OSINT). This decomposition turns a vague ask into a targeted plan, preventing the collect-everything hoarding that buries analysts.",
"A collection management framework tracks which requirements are covered by which sources, where the gaps are, and where sources overlap redundantly. It prioritizes — finite collection capacity goes to the highest-priority requirements first — and de-conflicts, so effort is not wasted collecting the same thing three ways while a critical gap goes unfilled. This discipline is what keeps a CTI program's collection aligned with what decision-makers actually need, and its absence is why under-managed programs simultaneously drown in some data and starve for other."],
ex:"A CTI lead decomposes the requirement understand our ransomware risk into six specific collection needs, maps each to sources, and finds that five are well-covered but one — the groups' current initial-access techniques — has no reliable source. Collection is retasked to fill that gap (adding a specialized feed and internal phishing telemetry) rather than piling more onto the already-covered needs. The framework directed effort to the gap, not the surplus.",
refs:[["Mandiant / SANS, collection management frameworks","Mapping requirements to sources and managing gaps."],["NIST SP 800-150","Aligning information collection with defensive needs."]],
kc:[
{q:"Collection management decomposes a broad requirement into:",o:["A single feed purchase","Specific collection needs, each mapped to sources that can satisfy it","An attribution","A report"],a:1,x:"Decomposing the question into targeted needs and sources prevents collect-everything hoarding and focuses effort."},
{q:"A collection management framework primarily tracks:",o:["Only budget","Which requirements are covered by which sources, plus gaps and redundant overlaps","Malware hashes","Employee schedules"],a:1,x:"It maps coverage, exposes gaps, and de-conflicts overlaps so finite collection serves the highest-priority requirements."},
{q:"An under-managed collection program tends to:",o:["Perfectly cover all needs","Simultaneously drown in some data while starving for other critical intelligence","Collect nothing","Only use OSINT"],a:1,x:"Without prioritization and gap tracking, effort piles onto covered needs while important gaps go unfilled."}]},
{t:"Threat Intelligence Platforms and Enrichment",d:"Managing, enriching, and integrating intelligence at scale.",
obj:["Explain the role of a Threat Intelligence Platform (TIP)","Describe enrichment and its value","Explain integrating CTI with security tooling"],
read:["At scale, threat intelligence must be managed by tooling. A Threat Intelligence Platform (TIP) ingests intelligence from many sources (feeds, ISAC shares, internal analysis), normalizes and deduplicates it, stores it with context and confidence, and integrates it with the security stack — pushing indicators to the SIEM, EDR, and firewalls, and pulling internal telemetry back for correlation. The TIP is what turns disparate intelligence streams into a coherent, queryable, operational resource rather than a pile of overlapping feeds.",
"Enrichment is the value-adding step the TIP enables: taking a bare indicator and adding context — what actor it is associated with, what TTPs, its confidence and age, whether it has ever appeared in your own environment. Enriched intelligence is what analysts and automated systems can actually act on with appropriate confidence, and it is the practical realization of the data-to-intelligence transformation from module three. Integration closes the loop: enriched, high-confidence indicators automatically inform detection and blocking, while the TIP tracks which intelligence produced hits — feeding the impact measurement that judges the program. Without a TIP, this operationalization is manual, inconsistent, and does not scale."],
ex:"A TIP ingests a new indicator, enriches it with the associated actor, TTPs, and a confidence score, checks it against 90 days of internal logs (finding one historical hit), and — because confidence is high — automatically pushes it to the EDR for blocking while flagging the historical hit for investigation. The platform turned a bare indicator into an enriched, actioned, tracked piece of intelligence at machine speed.",
refs:[["MISP and commercial TIP documentation","Threat intelligence platform capabilities and enrichment."],["SANS, integrating CTI with the security stack","Operationalizing enriched intelligence through tooling."]],
kc:[
{q:"A Threat Intelligence Platform (TIP) primarily:",o:["Replaces analysts","Ingests, normalizes, enriches, stores, and integrates intelligence with the security stack at scale","Encrypts the network","Writes strategic reports"],a:1,x:"The TIP turns disparate streams into a coherent, queryable, operational resource integrated with detection and blocking."},
{q:"Enrichment adds value to an indicator by:",o:["Deleting its context","Adding associated actor, TTPs, confidence, age, and whether it appeared in your environment","Making it a hash","Hiding it"],a:1,x:"Enrichment is the data-to-intelligence transformation in practice — context and confidence that make an indicator actionable."},
{q:"Integrating a TIP with the security stack enables:",o:["Manual, inconsistent blocking","Automatic detection/blocking from high-confidence intelligence plus tracking of which intelligence produced hits","Ignoring internal telemetry","Slower response"],a:1,x:"Integration operationalizes enriched intelligence at machine speed and feeds the impact measurement that judges the program."}]},
{t:"CTI Capstone",d:"Tracing an actor and producing actionable intelligence.",
obj:["Synthesize the lifecycle, frameworks, and operationalization into one analysis","Produce multi-level intelligence products from a single investigation","Judge CTI quality by rigor and defensive impact"],
read:["This module runs a CTI investigation end to end. Starting from a requirement (which actor threatens our sector and how do we defend?), the analyst collects OSINT and shared intelligence, processes and enriches the data, and analyzes it using the frameworks: mapping the actor's behavior to ATT&CK, sequencing it on the Kill Chain, and pivoting across the Diamond Model to expand from initial indicators to a full campaign picture. Structured analytic techniques and honest confidence levels govern the analysis, and attribution is stated at the level the evidence supports.",
"The output is deliberately multi-level: a strategic briefing for leadership (the actor's sustained targeting and its business implication), an operational profile for the hunt team (the campaign's TTPs), and tactical products for the SOC (ATT&CK-mapped detections and high-value indicators emphasizing the top of the Pyramid of Pain). Each is pitched to its audience, and each is designed to change defense — detections that fire, hunts that look for the right techniques, decisions that close real gaps. Feedback then refines the next requirement, closing the lifecycle.",
"The capstone's lesson is that CTI quality is judged by rigor and impact together: an analysis that is well-sourced, structurally sound, honestly caveated, and — above all — operationalized into defensive action. The graded deliverable, tracing a real-world actor, mapping to ATT&CK, and extracting actionable IOCs, exercises exactly this: not a report for its own sake, but intelligence that a SOC could act on tomorrow, built with the discipline that separates genuine intelligence from a pile of feeds.",],
ex:"An analyst investigates a group targeting the sector: OSINT and ISAC data feed an ATT&CK mapping and Diamond-Model pivoting that reveal the full campaign; the analysis, caveated at moderate-confidence attribution, yields a leadership briefing, a hunt-team TTP profile, and SIEM detections built on the group's durable techniques rather than perishable hashes. Weeks later a detection fires on a real intrusion attempt — the intelligence changed defense, the measure of success.",
refs:[["MITRE ATT&CK, Diamond Model, Kill Chain","The frameworks applied together in a full analysis."],["SANS FOR578, Cyber Threat Intelligence","The end-to-end CTI analytic process."]],
kc:[
{q:"A single CTI investigation should typically produce:",o:["One tactical blocklist only","Multi-level products — strategic, operational, and tactical — each pitched to its audience","A report no one reads","Only an attribution"],a:1,x:"The same analysis serves leadership (strategic), hunters (operational), and the SOC (tactical), each in an actionable form."},
{q:"In the capstone, detections should be built preferentially on:",o:["Perishable hashes and IPs","Durable, high-Pyramid-of-Pain techniques (TTPs) that resist adversary evasion","Random indicators","The actor's name"],a:1,x:"Technique-based detections survive the adversary's tool and infrastructure changes, unlike easily-rotated hashes and IPs."},
{q:"CTI quality in the capstone is judged by:",o:["Report length and feed count","Analytic rigor combined with operational impact — intelligence a SOC could act on","Attribution certainty alone","The number of frameworks named"],a:1,x:"Well-sourced, structurally sound, honestly caveated, and operationalized — rigor plus defensive impact define quality CTI."}]}
],
cap:[
{q:"SCENARIO: A CTI team ingests dozens of feeds but produces reports no one acts on. The root problem is:",o:["Too few feeds","No clear requirements driving the cycle and no operationalization into defensive action","Too much analysis","Weak encryption"],a:1,x:"Without requirements and operationalization, the program hoards data and produces unused reports — activity without impact."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An analyst wants to deliver raw IOCs to the executive board. This fails because:",o:["Executives cannot read","It confuses the intelligence level — executives need the strategic implication, not tactical indicators","IOCs are secret","Boards never meet"],a:1,x:"Level confusion wastes intelligence; the board needs the business-relevant strategic message, while IOCs belong with the SOC."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A team blocks a malware hash and its C2 IP; the adversary defeats both within hours. The durable alternative was:",o:["Blocking more hashes","An ATT&CK-based detection for the technique the malware used, which survives tool/infrastructure changes","Attribution","A longer blocklist"],a:1,x:"High-Pyramid-of-Pain, behavior-based detection catches the adversary's next sample; low-pyramid IOCs are trivially rotated."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An analyst states 'this was definitely Group X' with no caveats, and is later proven wrong. The failure was:",o:["Attributing at all","Overconfident attribution — it should have carried an explicit, evidence-based confidence level","Using ATT&CK","Sharing the finding"],a:1,x:"Responsible attribution states confidence and evidence; unqualified certainty misdirects defense and erodes credibility when wrong."},
{q:"SCENARIO: Starting from one malicious domain, an analyst finds the malware, the group, and three other victims. This is:",o:["Confirmation bias","Diamond-Model pivoting — using one feature to discover connected features across a campaign","Attribution","Random luck"],a:1,x:"Pivoting across the diamond's edges expands a single observation into a campaign-level picture."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A team shares a phishing campaign's indicators via its ISAC marked TLP:AMBER. This means:",o:["Publish it publicly","Share within the trusted community but not publicly","Delete it immediately","Send only to one named person"],a:1,x:"TLP:AMBER limits redistribution to the community; the marking balances sharing value against protecting sensitive details."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An analyst fixates on their first-impression attribution until a structured technique reveals a better-fitting hypothesis. The bias overcome was:",o:["Availability bias","Anchoring/confirmation bias, countered by Analysis of Competing Hypotheses","Mirror-imaging only","No bias existed"],a:1,x:"Anchoring on the first indicator is corrected by explicitly weighing competing hypotheses against the evidence."},
{q:"SCENARIO: Ransomware analysis yields detections, a hunt that finds a dormant intrusion, and a funded MFA rollout. The program's value is measured by:",o:["The report's page count","These defensive outcomes — a caught intrusion and a closed gap","The number of feeds ingested","Attribution confidence"],a:1,x:"CTI is measured by operational impact, not activity; outcomes that change defense are the success metric."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A defender wants to know what an attacker could learn about the organization from public sources. The relevant discipline is:",o:["Reverse engineering","OSINT — enumerating exposed services, employees, and leaked credentials from public data","Cryptography","Patch management"],a:1,x:"OSINT reveals the organization's public attack surface, informing attack-surface reduction — the dual-use lens."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A raw feed of a million IPs is dropped into a blocklist, causing false positives and no insight. The lesson is:",o:["Buy more feeds","A feed is data, not intelligence — it requires enrichment, relevance, and confidence analysis to be actionable","Block fewer IPs","Feeds are useless"],a:1,x:"Equating feeds with intelligence skips the analytic transformation; unprocessed data produces noise, not decisions."}
]};
